#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""dup-const-lint (#222) - ONE NAME, ONE VALUE.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

The most expensive recurring fault in this project is not a hard bug. It is a
value written down twice. Someone fixes one copy, the fix reads correct in
review, and the shipped binary reads the other copy. The record is unambiguous:

  #106   FIVE version.h files each defining MAYTERA_VERSION_STRING differently,
         all behind the SAME include guard, so whichever was included first won
         and the other four were invisible.
  #178   FIVE copies of the #145 fix. The fifth still carried the pid-indexed
         baseline that #145 believed it had removed.
  #166   a SIXTH CPU-count constant, BKL_STAT_CPUS=8, read out of bounds, when
         five copies were already known and thought to be all of them.
  #745   two branches allocated syscall 373; git merged both without a conflict
         because the #defines sat at different line numbers. It COMPILED.
         Renumbering then reached four of the five copies, and the fifth (a
         no_std Rust app that cannot include the C header) shipped calling
         SYS_WM_SET_WORK_AREA believing it was a login-mode setter.
  #222   dock geometry: DOCK_HEIGHT_MIN/MAX/DEFAULT and DOCK_ZOOM_MIN/MAX/DEFAULT
         defined in BOTH userland/apps/settings/main.c and
         userland/apps/compositor/taskbar.c, held in sync by a source comment
         reading "must match taskbar.c".

That comment is the whole problem in one line. A comment is not a mechanism. It
cannot fail, it cannot block a build, and it is read only by someone who already
suspects there is a second copy.

THE MECHANISM, NOT THE INSTANCE. Fixing the dock constants fixes today. The
seventh copy of the seventh constant gets introduced the same way tomorrow. So:

  A named constant may be defined with only ONE value in the tree. Define it
  twice with different values and the BUILD FAILS, unless the divergence is
  written down in allowlist.tsv with a reason.

WHAT IT READS

Exactly two forms, and only when the value is a LITERAL:

    C/C++/headers   #define NAME <literal>
    Rust            [pub] const NAME: T = <literal>;
                    [pub] static [mut] NAME: T = <literal>;

A "literal" is an integer (dec/hex/octal/binary, any C or Rust suffix, any
number of redundant parens), a character constant, a string constant, or a
float. Anything else - an expression, a reference to another macro, a cast, a
sizeof, a function-like macro - is NOT COMPARED by the automatic rules.

That narrowness is the design, not a shortcut. `#define BUF (CHUNK * 4)` in two
files is the same TEXT and possibly two different VALUES; `#define BUF 4096` and
`#define BUF 0x1000` are different text and certainly the same value. A gate
that guesses at expressions produces a list nobody reads, and a gate nobody
reads gets allowlisted into decoration. Detecting the literal case PERFECTLY is
worth more than a heuristic that flags three hundred things.

Values are NORMALISED before comparison, so 44 == 0x2C == ((44u)) and the gate
never cries wolf over a base change.

THE THREE RULES, AND WHY ONLY TWO OF THEM CAN FAIL A BUILD

Measured on the tree at #222: 138 names are defined with differing values. That
number is useless as a gate; it splits, cleanly and 69/69, along one line.

  RULE A - SHARED (69 names on the current tree). At least one of the
  definitions lives in a HEADER. GATING.
      A #define in a .h is by intent a contract offered to other files. A
      second, different definition of that name anywhere is a conflict, and
      which one wins depends on include order - which is precisely how #106
      hid five different MAYTERA_VERSION_STRINGs behind one include guard.
      This class is dense with real faults: it found libc's fb_get_info()
      calling SYS_DUP, a kernel/userland struct-termios size split, two
      THEME_NAME_LEN buffer sizes, and two JPEG size ceilings.

  RULE B - PRIVATE (69 names on the current tree). Every definition is in a
  .c or .rs. REPORTED, NEVER GATING.
      A #define in a .c has translation-unit scope by language rule. Two apps
      each writing `#define FOOTER_H 28` and `#define FOOTER_H 24` are two
      private numbers in two separate binaries, and they always will be. The
      current 69 are almost entirely per-app layout (BTN_H, SIDEBAR_W, PAD, W,
      MAXH). Failing on those is exactly the cry-wolf that gets a gate
      allowlisted into decoration, so this rule does not fail. It prints a
      count, and --report lists them.

  RULE C - DECLARED VALUE CONTRACTS (contracts.tsv). Opt-in. GATING.
      Rules A and B are anchored on the NAME, and the fault that opened #222
      HAS NO SHARED NAME: settings/main.c calls it DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT and
      compositor/taskbar.c calls it XFCE_DOCK_H_DEFAULT. No name-anchored
      check can ever see that pair, and saying so is more useful than a gate
      that quietly does not cover its own motivating bug.
      So a contract is declared by VALUE and by SITE, not by name: "these
      named constants, in these files, must all equal 59". Sites are resolved
      by evaluating the constant, following same-file macro references
      (XFCE_DOCK_H_DEFAULT is a sum of four other macros), so a site does not
      have to be a bare literal. A site that drifts FAILS. A site that no
      longer exists FAILS, because a contract describing something that is not
      there has stopped describing the thing that is.

WHAT IT CANNOT CATCH (read this before trusting it)

  - A duplicated EXPRESSION under rules A/B. Deliberate, per the scope note.
  - Differently-named duplicates that nobody has declared in contracts.tsv.
    Rule C is the mechanism, but it only covers what is written down. There
    is no automatic detection here and there cannot be one: nothing textual
    can know that DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT and XFCE_DOCK_H_DEFAULT are one number.
  - A value duplicated between source and a NON-SOURCE artifact: a shell
    script, a Makefile, an asset, a partition offset baked into a .sh.
  - An enum member, or a bare literal used inline, versus a #define of the
    same concept. DOOM's i_video.c calls `syscall1(201, ...)` with the name
    only in a comment; nothing here sees that, and argtab.rs has the same
    shape (which is why #745 needed its own dedicated lint).
  - The same name meaning two genuinely UNRELATED things in two subsystems
    (R_OK is a POSIX access() mode in libc and a local refusal code in
    rustkern/fetchown.rs). That is a real false positive, and allowlist.tsv,
    with a written reason per name, is where it is recorded.
  - A twin FILE (hda.c live, intel_hda.c an unused twin; main.c beside
    main.rs). See --twins and read the honest limit documented there.

USAGE
  dup-const-lint <root> [--dirs kernel] [--strict] [--twins]
  dup-const-lint --self-test        # PROVE it goes RED on a duplicate, GREEN on a clean tree
  dup-const-lint <root> --report    # ungated inventory of every divergence, allowlisted or not

Exit 0 = clean, 1 = a divergence is not allowlisted, a contract is broken, or an
allowlist/contract entry is stale or unjustified; 2 = the lint could not run
(fail closed).
"""

import os
import re
import sys
import subprocess

TOOLDIR = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
ALLOWLIST = os.path.join(TOOLDIR, "allowlist.tsv")
CONTRACTS = os.path.join(TOOLDIR, "contracts.tsv")
SCOPE = os.path.join(TOOLDIR, "scope.conf")

C_EXT = (".c", ".h", ".cpp", ".hpp", ".cc", ".cxx", ".hh")
RS_EXT = (".rs",)
HDR_EXT = (".h", ".hpp", ".hh")

VALID_STATUS = ("[LEGIT]", "[DEBT]", "[LEGACY]")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- value parsing
_INT_RE = re.compile(
    r"""^ [+-]? (?:
            0[xX][0-9a-fA-F_]+
          | 0[bB][01_]+
          | 0[oO][0-7_]+
          | [0-9][0-9_]*
        ) $""",
    re.X,
)
_INTSUF_C = re.compile(r"(?:[uU](?:LL|ll|L|l)?|(?:LL|ll|L|l)[uU]?)$")
_INTSUF_RS = re.compile(r"(?:_?(?:[iu](?:8|16|32|64|128|size)))$")
_FLT_RE = re.compile(r"^[+-]?(?:\d[\d_]*\.\d[\d_]*|\d[\d_]*\.|\.\d[\d_]*)(?:[eE][+-]?\d+)?$")
_FLTSUF = re.compile(r"(?:[fFlL]|_?f(?:32|64))$")
_STR_RE = re.compile(r'^"(?:[^"\\]|\\.)*"$')
_CHR_RE = re.compile(r"^'(?:[^'\\]|\\.)*'$")


def strip_parens(s):
    """((44u)) -> 44u. Only strips when the outer pair actually wraps the whole
    token, so (a)+(b) is left alone and therefore rejected as a non-literal."""
    s = s.strip()
    while len(s) >= 2 and s[0] == "(" and s[-1] == ")":
        depth = 0
        wraps = True
        for i, ch in enumerate(s):
            if ch == "(":
                depth += 1
            elif ch == ")":
                depth -= 1
                if depth == 0 and i != len(s) - 1:
                    wraps = False
                    break
        if not wraps:
            break
        s = s[1:-1].strip()
    return s


def normalise(raw):
    """Return a canonical comparable form, or None if this is not a literal.

    None is not a failure. It means 'this value is an expression and this lint
    has nothing trustworthy to say about it', which is the whole scope decision.
    """
    s = strip_parens(raw)
    if not s:
        return None

    if _STR_RE.match(s):
        return "str:" + s[1:-1]
    if _CHR_RE.match(s):
        return "chr:" + s[1:-1]

    # integer, after peeling one C or Rust suffix
    t = s
    for suf in (_INTSUF_RS, _INTSUF_C):
        m = suf.search(t)
        if m and _INT_RE.match(t[: m.start()].replace("_", "") or "x"):
            t = t[: m.start()]
            break
    t_clean = t.replace("_", "")
    if _INT_RE.match(t_clean):
        neg = t_clean.startswith("-")
        body = t_clean.lstrip("+-")
        try:
            if body[:2] in ("0x", "0X"):
                v = int(body, 16)
            elif body[:2] in ("0b", "0B"):
                v = int(body, 2)
            elif body[:2] in ("0o", "0O"):
                v = int(body[2:], 8)
            elif len(body) > 1 and body[0] == "0":
                v = int(body, 8)
            else:
                v = int(body, 10)
        except ValueError:
            return None
        return "int:%d" % (-v if neg else v)

    # float, after peeling a suffix
    t = s
    m = _FLTSUF.search(t)
    if m and _FLT_RE.match(t[: m.start()].replace("_", "")):
        t = t[: m.start()]
    if _FLT_RE.match(t.replace("_", "")):
        try:
            return "flt:%r" % float(t.replace("_", ""))
        except ValueError:
            return None
    return None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- source parsing
_DEFINE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*#\s*define\s+([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)(.*)$")
_RSCONST_RE = re.compile(
    r"^\s*(?:pub(?:\s*\([^)]*\))?\s+)?(?:const|static)\s+(?:mut\s+)?"
    r"([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*:\s*[^=;]+=\s*(.+?)\s*;\s*$"
)


def strip_trailing_comment(s):
    # Only handles the trailing case, which is all a single-line define can have
    # after the value. A // or /* inside a string literal must not be cut.
    out = []
    i = 0
    instr = None
    while i < len(s):
        ch = s[i]
        if instr:
            if ch == "\\":
                out.append(s[i : i + 2])
                i += 2
                continue
            if ch == instr:
                instr = None
            out.append(ch)
            i += 1
            continue
        if ch in "\"'":
            instr = ch
            out.append(ch)
            i += 1
            continue
        if s.startswith("//", i) or s.startswith("/*", i):
            break
        out.append(ch)
        i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def scan_file(path, rel):
    """Yield (name, norm, raw, rel, lineno)."""
    ext = os.path.splitext(path)[1]
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            lines = fh.read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return
    if ext in C_EXT:
        for n, line in enumerate(lines, 1):
            if line.rstrip().endswith("\\"):
                continue  # multi-line macro: not a literal by definition
            m = _DEFINE_RE.match(line)
            if not m:
                continue
            name, rest = m.group(1), m.group(2)
            if rest.startswith("("):
                continue  # function-like macro
            raw = strip_trailing_comment(rest).strip()
            if not raw:
                continue  # bare #define / include guard
            norm = normalise(raw)
            if norm is not None:
                yield (name, norm, raw, rel, n)
    elif ext in RS_EXT:
        for n, line in enumerate(lines, 1):
            m = _RSCONST_RE.match(line)
            if not m:
                continue
            name, raw = m.group(1), strip_trailing_comment(m.group(2)).strip()
            if not raw:
                continue
            norm = normalise(raw)
            if norm is not None:
                yield (name, norm, raw, rel, n)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- scope
def load_scope(root, strict, errs):
    """Excluded path prefixes. Each must still exist: a stale exclusion silently
    widens or narrows the gate, and this tree has been bitten by stale rulebook
    entries before (invariant-gate allowlist, KNOWN_STALE_LAUNCH)."""
    prefixes = []
    if not os.path.exists(SCOPE):
        errs.append("scope.conf missing beside the lint; refusing to guess at scope")
        return None
    with open(SCOPE, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        for n, line in enumerate(fh, 1):
            line = line.split("#", 1)[0].strip()
            if not line:
                continue
            p = line.rstrip("/")
            if strict and not os.path.exists(os.path.join(root, p)):
                errs.append("scope.conf:%d STALE: '%s' does not exist under %s" % (n, p, root))
            prefixes.append(p)
    return prefixes


def in_scope(rel, prefixes):
    for p in prefixes:
        if rel == p or rel.startswith(p + "/"):
            return False
    return True


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- allowlist
def load_allowlist(errs):
    """name<TAB>status<TAB>reason. Status must be [LEGIT]/[DEBT]/[LEGACY] and the
    reason must be real prose, exactly as kernel/tools/concurrency-lint requires.
    An entry with no justification is a silencer, not a decision."""
    allow = {}
    if not os.path.exists(ALLOWLIST):
        errs.append("allowlist.tsv missing beside the lint")
        return allow
    with open(ALLOWLIST, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        for n, line in enumerate(fh, 1):
            if line.startswith("#") or not line.strip():
                continue
            parts = line.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
            if len(parts) < 3:
                errs.append("allowlist.tsv:%d malformed (need name<TAB>status<TAB>reason)" % n)
                continue
            name, status, reason = parts[0].strip(), parts[1].strip(), "\t".join(parts[2:]).strip()
            if status not in VALID_STATUS:
                errs.append(
                    "allowlist.tsv:%d '%s' has status '%s'; must be one of %s"
                    % (n, name, status, " ".join(VALID_STATUS))
                )
                continue
            if len(reason) < 25:
                errs.append(
                    "allowlist.tsv:%d '%s' has no real justification (%d chars). "
                    "An entry without a reason is a silencer." % (n, name, len(reason))
                )
                continue
            if name in allow:
                errs.append("allowlist.tsv:%d duplicate entry for '%s'" % (n, name))
                continue
            allow[name] = (status, reason)
    return allow


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- contracts
#
# Rule C. Everything below exists for one reason: the fault that opened #222 has
# NO SHARED NAME (DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT vs XFCE_DOCK_H_DEFAULT), so it is
# structurally invisible to a name-anchored check. A contract names the SITES
# instead, and asserts the VALUE they must all carry.

_TOK_RE = re.compile(r"\s*(0[xX][0-9a-fA-F]+|\d+|[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*|<<|>>|[-+*/%()~|&^])")


def eval_const(name, table, depth=0, seen=None):
    """Evaluate a constant to an int by following same-file macro references.

    Used ONLY by rule C, never by rules A/B. A contract is opt-in, so a value
    this cannot evaluate is a loud FAIL rather than a silent skip: the failure
    mode of a gate that quietly stops checking is the one this whole ticket is
    about.

    Deliberately arithmetic-only and same-file-only. It is not a preprocessor
    and must not grow into one; anything it cannot fold, it refuses.
    """
    if depth > 12:
        return None, "macro expansion too deep at '%s'" % name
    seen = seen or set()
    if name in seen:
        return None, "circular macro reference at '%s'" % name
    if name not in table:
        return None, "no definition of '%s' in that file" % name
    raw = table[name]
    toks = []
    pos = 0
    while pos < len(raw):
        m = _TOK_RE.match(raw, pos)
        if not m:
            return None, "cannot tokenise '%s' = %s" % (name, raw)
        toks.append(m.group(1))
        pos = m.end()
    out = []
    for t in toks:
        if re.match(r"^[A-Za-z_]", t):
            sub, err = eval_const(t, table, depth + 1, seen | {name})
            if sub is None:
                return None, err
            out.append("(%d)" % sub)
        else:
            out.append(t)
    expr = "".join(out)
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9xXa-fA-F()+\-*/%<>|&^~ ]+", expr):
        return None, "unsafe expression for '%s': %s" % (name, raw)
    try:
        v = eval(expr, {"__builtins__": {}}, {})  # noqa: S307 - grammar checked above
    except Exception as exc:  # pragma: no cover - defensive
        return None, "cannot evaluate '%s' = %s (%s)" % (name, raw, exc)
    if not isinstance(v, int):
        return None, "'%s' did not evaluate to an integer" % name
    return v, None


def file_define_table(path):
    """Every #define NAME <rest> in one file, as raw text. Rule C only."""
    table = {}
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            lines = fh.read().splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None
    ext = os.path.splitext(path)[1]
    for line in lines:
        if line.rstrip().endswith("\\"):
            continue
        if ext in C_EXT:
            m = _DEFINE_RE.match(line)
            if not m or m.group(2).startswith("("):
                # a leading "(" is ambiguous: a function-like macro, or a
                # parenthesised value. Only the no-space form is function-like.
                if not m or re.match(r"^\([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*\s*[,)]", m.group(2)):
                    continue
            raw = strip_trailing_comment(m.group(2)).strip()
            if raw:
                table.setdefault(m.group(1), raw)
        elif ext in RS_EXT:
            m = _RSCONST_RE.match(line)
            if m:
                raw = strip_trailing_comment(m.group(2)).strip()
                if raw:
                    table.setdefault(m.group(1), raw)
    return table


def load_contracts(errs):
    """contract<TAB>value<TAB>file:NAME[;file:NAME...]<TAB>why

    Same justification discipline as the allowlist: a contract with no stated
    reason is a number somebody typed, and the next person has no way to judge
    whether it is still true."""
    out = []
    if not os.path.exists(CONTRACTS):
        errs.append("contracts.tsv missing beside the lint")
        return out
    with open(CONTRACTS, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        for n, line in enumerate(fh, 1):
            if line.startswith("#") or not line.strip():
                continue
            parts = line.rstrip("\n").split("\t")
            if len(parts) < 4:
                errs.append("contracts.tsv:%d malformed (need name<TAB>value<TAB>sites<TAB>why)" % n)
                continue
            cname, val, sites, why = (p.strip() for p in parts[:4])
            try:
                ival = int(val, 0)
            except ValueError:
                errs.append("contracts.tsv:%d '%s' value '%s' is not an integer" % (n, cname, val))
                continue
            sl = [s.strip() for s in sites.split(";") if s.strip()]
            if len(sl) < 2:
                errs.append("contracts.tsv:%d '%s' names %d site(s); a contract needs at least 2"
                            % (n, cname, len(sl)))
                continue
            if len(why) < 25:
                errs.append("contracts.tsv:%d '%s' has no real justification" % (n, cname))
                continue
            out.append((n, cname, ival, sl, why))
    return out


def check_contracts(root, contracts, errs):
    tables = {}
    for n, cname, ival, sites, _why in contracts:
        seen = []
        for site in sites:
            if ":" not in site:
                errs.append("contracts.tsv:%d '%s' site '%s' is not file:NAME" % (n, cname, site))
                continue
            rel, sym = site.rsplit(":", 1)
            path = os.path.join(root, rel)
            if rel not in tables:
                tables[rel] = file_define_table(path)
            tab = tables[rel]
            if tab is None:
                errs.append("contracts.tsv:%d '%s' STALE: %s does not exist. A contract "
                            "describing a file that is gone has stopped describing anything."
                            % (n, cname, rel))
                continue
            v, err = eval_const(sym, tab)
            if v is None:
                errs.append("contracts.tsv:%d '%s' site %s: %s" % (n, cname, site, err))
                continue
            seen.append((site, v))
        for site, v in seen:
            if v != ival:
                errs.append("contracts.tsv:%d CONTRACT BROKEN '%s': %s is %d, contract says %d"
                            % (n, cname, site, v, ival))
    return


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- twins
_FUNCDEF_RE = re.compile(
    r"^(?!static\b|typedef\b|extern\b|return\b|else\b|case\b|#)"
    r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_ \t*]*?\b([a-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\s*\([^;]*$"
)
_IDENT_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*")


def find_twins(root, files):
    """Report source files that NOTHING ELSE REFERENCES.

    THE HEURISTIC I TRIED FIRST AND THREW AWAY, recorded so nobody rebuilds it:
    "X.c beside <prefix>X.c that DEFINE THE SAME CONSTANT NAMES". It sounds
    right and it finds NOTHING. Measured on this tree: kernel/drivers/hda.h and
    kernel/drivers/intel_hda.h - the canonical live/dead twin pair, and the
    example the ticket names - share ZERO define names (196 and 78 of them, no
    overlap), because each prefixes its own. Name similarity without that
    corroboration would flag every x.c/prefix_x.c pair in the tree.

    "Compiled by nothing" is also not the question. kernel/Makefile collects
    sources with $(wildcard drivers/*.c), so intel_hda.c IS compiled. It was an
    unused twin because nothing CALLED it.

    So that is what this measures, directly: a .c file that defines at least one
    non-static function and NONE of those names appears in any other in-scope
    file. On the real tree intel_hda.c comes out with zero external references
    for all 19 of its exported functions, while hda.c's are called from
    audio.c - which is exactly the live/dead distinction, obtained without
    guessing at names.

    REPORT ONLY, never gating, for one honest reason: a function reached solely
    from assembly, from a function-pointer table built by string name, or from a
    Rust FFI declaration this parser does not read, looks identical to a dead
    one. It is a lead, not a verdict.
    """
    exports = {}
    idents = {}
    for rel in files:
        path = os.path.join(root, rel)
        try:
            with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
                text = fh.read()
        except OSError:
            continue
        idents[rel] = set(_IDENT_RE.findall(text))
        if not rel.endswith(".c"):
            continue
        fns = set()
        for line in text.splitlines():
            m = _FUNCDEF_RE.match(line)
            if m:
                fns.add(m.group(1))
        if fns:
            exports[rel] = fns
    out = []
    for rel, fns in sorted(exports.items()):
        referenced = False
        for other, ids in idents.items():
            if other == rel or os.path.splitext(other)[0] == os.path.splitext(rel)[0]:
                continue  # its own companion header does not count as a user
            if fns & ids:
                referenced = True
                break
        if not referenced:
            out.append((rel, len(fns)))
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- driver
def list_files(root, dirs):
    """Tracked files only. git ls-files is the source of truth for what is IN the
    tree; a raw walk picks up build output, agent scratch and stale copies, and
    those would make the verdict depend on how dirty the checkout happens to be."""
    cmd = ["git", "-C", root, "ls-files", "-z"]
    if dirs:
        cmd += ["--"] + list(dirs)
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, check=True).stdout
        rels = [r.decode("utf-8", "replace") for r in out.split(b"\0") if r]
    except (OSError, subprocess.CalledProcessError):
        rels = []
        bases = [os.path.join(root, d) for d in dirs] if dirs else [root]
        for base in bases:
            for dp, dn, fn in os.walk(base):
                dn[:] = [x for x in dn if x != ".git"]
                for f in fn:
                    rels.append(os.path.relpath(os.path.join(dp, f), root))
    return [r for r in rels if os.path.splitext(r)[1] in C_EXT + RS_EXT]


def collect(root, dirs, prefixes):
    defs = {}
    files = []
    for rel in list_files(root, dirs):
        if not in_scope(rel, prefixes):
            continue
        files.append(rel)
        for name, norm, raw, r, n in scan_file(os.path.join(root, rel), rel):
            defs.setdefault(name, []).append((norm, raw, r, n))
    return defs, files


def divergences(defs):
    """A name diverges when it holds >=2 distinct values AND those values do not
    all come from a single file. Same-file alternatives are #ifdef branches."""
    out = []
    for name, ds in defs.items():
        vals = {}
        for norm, raw, rel, n in ds:
            vals.setdefault(norm, []).append((rel, n, raw))
        if len(vals) < 2:
            continue
        files = {rel for _, _, rel, _ in ds}
        if len(files) < 2:
            continue
        # require two DIFFERENT files to disagree, not just two values present
        disagree = False
        for v1 in vals:
            for v2 in vals:
                if v1 == v2:
                    continue
                if {f for f, _, _ in vals[v1]} - {f for f, _, _ in vals[v2]}:
                    disagree = True
        if disagree:
            out.append((name, vals))
    return sorted(out)


def is_shared(vals):
    """Rule A vs rule B: does any definition live in a header?"""
    for v in vals:
        for rel, _n, _raw in vals[v]:
            if os.path.splitext(rel)[1] in HDR_EXT:
                return True
    return False


def fmt(name, vals):
    lines = ["  %s" % name]
    for norm in sorted(vals):
        for rel, n, raw in sorted(vals[norm]):
            lines.append("      %-14s %s:%d   (%s)" % (raw, rel, n, norm))
    return "\n".join(lines)


def run(root, dirs, strict, twins, report):
    errs = []
    prefixes = load_scope(root, strict, errs)
    if prefixes is None:
        for e in errs:
            print("[dup-const-lint] FATAL: %s" % e, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    allow = load_allowlist(errs)
    contracts = load_contracts(errs)
    defs, files = collect(root, dirs, prefixes)
    if not files:
        print("[dup-const-lint] FATAL: no source files in scope under %s" % root, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    divs = divergences(defs)
    shared = [(n, v) for n, v in divs if is_shared(v)]
    private = [(n, v) for n, v in divs if not is_shared(v)]
    unallowed = [(n, v) for n, v in shared if n not in allow]
    seen_shared = {n for n, _ in shared}
    stale = sorted(n for n in allow if n not in seen_shared)

    print("[dup-const-lint] scanned %d files, %d literal-valued names; "
          "%d divergent (%d shared/gating, %d private/reported)"
          % (len(files), len(defs), len(divs), len(shared), len(private)))
    if report:
        print("\n--- RULE A: SHARED (a header defines it) - THESE GATE THE BUILD ---")
        for name, vals in shared:
            tag = " [allowlisted %s]" % allow[name][0] if name in allow else "   <-- NOT ALLOWLISTED"
            print(fmt(name, vals) + tag)
        print("\n--- RULE B: PRIVATE (.c/.rs only) - reported, never gating ---")
        for name, vals in private:
            print(fmt(name, vals))
        return 0

    # Rule C runs against the whole tree only. Under --dirs the other files are
    # not present, so a contract site would look "missing" and fail spuriously.
    if not dirs:
        check_contracts(root, contracts, errs)
    elif strict:
        errs.append("--strict with --dirs cannot check contracts.tsv; run it over the whole tree")

    if private:
        print("[dup-const-lint] note: %d name(s) diverge only between .c/.rs files "
              "(translation-unit private; see --report, rule B)" % len(private))

    # STALE-ENTRY RULE, and why it is conditional. An allowlist row describing a
    # divergence that no longer exists must FAIL: a rulebook describing things
    # that are not there has stopped describing the things that are. But under
    # --dirs the other half of the tree is not being read, so every
    # kernel-vs-userland row (TERM_COLOR_*, NCCS, NSIG, R_OK...) would look
    # stale and the gate would fail for a reason that is not true. So the rule
    # runs only over a WHOLE-TREE scan, which is the invocation repo-guard uses.
    if dirs:
        if stale:
            print("[dup-const-lint] note: %d allowlist row(s) not exercised under --dirs %s; "
                  "staleness is only judged on a whole-tree scan" % (len(stale), " ".join(dirs)))
    else:
        for n in stale:
            errs.append(
                "allowlist.tsv entry '%s' is STALE: it no longer diverges in the shared "
                "class. Remove it. A rulebook describing things that are not there stops "
                "describing the things that are." % n
            )

    if twins:
        t = find_twins(root, files)
        for rel, nfn in t:
            print("[dup-const-lint] UNREFERENCED? %s exports %d non-static function(s), "
                  "none of which any other in-scope file names" % (rel, nfn))
        print("[dup-const-lint] twin report: %d file(s) with no external reference "
              "(REPORT ONLY, never gating; a table- or asm-reached entry point looks "
              "the same from here)" % len(t))

    if unallowed:
        print("[dup-const-lint] FAIL: %d name(s) are defined in a HEADER and also, with a "
              "DIFFERENT value, somewhere else:" % len(unallowed), file=sys.stderr)
        for name, vals in unallowed:
            print(fmt(name, vals), file=sys.stderr)
        print("\n  Make it ONE definition, or add a row to\n  %s\n"
              "  as  name<TAB>[LEGIT|DEBT|LEGACY]<TAB>why these really are two "
              "different things." % ALLOWLIST, file=sys.stderr)
    for e in errs:
        print("[dup-const-lint] FAIL: %s" % e, file=sys.stderr)

    if unallowed or errs:
        return 1
    print("[dup-const-lint] ok: every shared literal constant has ONE value "
          "(%d allowlisted divergence(s), all justified; %d value contract(s) hold)"
          % (len(allow), len(contracts)))
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- self-test
def self_test():
    """Prove BOTH directions on synthetic fixtures. A gate nobody has watched go
    red is not a gate (#514: a lint sat in this tree for months, documented as a
    build guardrail, incapable of failing for two independent reasons)."""
    import tempfile
    import shutil

    passed = 0
    total = 0

    def case(desc, want_red, files, allow_rows=None):
        nonlocal passed, total
        total += 1
        d = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="dupconst-st-")
        try:
            for rel, body in files.items():
                p = os.path.join(d, rel)
                os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)
                with open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                    fh.write(body)
            prefixes = []
            allow = {}
            for row in allow_rows or []:
                allow[row[0]] = (row[1], row[2])
            defs = {}
            fl = []
            for dp, dn, fn in os.walk(d):
                for f in sorted(fn):
                    rel = os.path.relpath(os.path.join(dp, f), d)
                    if os.path.splitext(rel)[1] not in C_EXT + RS_EXT:
                        continue
                    fl.append(rel)
                    for name, norm, raw, r, n in scan_file(os.path.join(d, rel), rel):
                        defs.setdefault(name, []).append((norm, raw, r, n))
            divs = [(n, v) for n, v in divergences(defs) if is_shared(v)]
            red = bool([n for n, _ in divs if n not in allow])
            if red == want_red:
                print("  %-5s (correct)  %s" % ("RED" if red else "GREEN", desc))
                passed += 1
            else:
                print("  %-5s (WRONG!)   %s  [saw %s]" % ("RED" if red else "GREEN", desc,
                                                          [n for n, _ in divs]))
        finally:
            shutil.rmtree(d, ignore_errors=True)

    print("SELF-TEST: prove dup-const-lint goes RED on a real duplicate and GREEN otherwise.")

    # 1. RULE A: a header's constant contradicted elsewhere -> RED.
    case("header says 59, a .c says 44 (rule A)", True, {
        "gui/dock.h": "#define DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT 59\n",
        "compositor/taskbar.c": "#define DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT 44\n",
    })

    # 1b. RULE B: the SAME divergence between two .c files does NOT gate. This is
    #     the deliberate false-positive ceiling: a #define in a .c is
    #     translation-unit private, and 69 per-app layout constants on the real
    #     tree collide by name with no consequence whatsoever.
    case("two .c files disagree - private, must NOT gate (rule B)", False, {
        "settings/main.c": "#define FOOTER_H 28\n",
        "files/main.c": "#define FOOTER_H 24\n",
    })

    # 2. clean tree: same name, same value, two files -> GREEN (mirrored contract)
    case("same name, same value, two files", False, {
        "a/a.h": "#define DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT 59\n",
        "b/b.c": "#define DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT 59\n",
    })

    # 3. base change must NOT cry wolf: 44 == 0x2C == ((44u))
    case("44 vs 0x2C vs ((44u)) are the SAME value", False, {
        "a/a.h": "#define WIDTH 44\n",
        "b/b.c": "#define WIDTH 0x2C\n",
        "c/c.c": "#define WIDTH ((44u))\n",
    })

    # 4. #ifdef alternatives inside ONE file are legitimate -> GREEN
    case("#ifdef branches in a single file", False, {
        "a/a.h": "#ifdef BIG\n#define SLOTS 64\n#else\n#define SLOTS 8\n#endif\n",
    })

    # 5. #745's fifth copy: a Rust const disagreeing with the C header -> RED
    case("Rust const disagrees with the C #define (the #745 fifth copy)", True, {
        "kernel/proc/syscall.h": "#define SYS_SET_LOGIN_MODE 374\n",
        "userland/apps/setup/main.rs": "const SYS_SET_LOGIN_MODE: i64 = 373;\n",
    })

    # 6. #106: two version.h files, different strings -> RED
    case("two version.h with different MAYTERA_VERSION_STRING (#106)", True, {
        "kernel/version.h": '#define MAYTERA_VERSION_STRING "1.9.0"\n',
        "old/version.h": '#define MAYTERA_VERSION_STRING "1.5.0"\n',
    })

    # 7. expressions are OUT OF SCOPE and must never be flagged, same or different
    case("expression-valued macros are never compared", False, {
        "a/a.h": "#define BUF (CHUNK * 4)\n",
        "b/b.c": "#define BUF (OTHER * 8)\n",
    })

    # 8. function-like macros and include guards are not constants
    case("function-like macro and bare include guard ignored", False, {
        "a/a.h": "#ifndef A_H\n#define A_H\n#define MAX(a,b) ((a)>(b)?(a):(b))\n#endif\n",
        "b/b.h": "#ifndef B_H\n#define B_H\n#define MAX(a,b) ((a)<(b)?(a):(b))\n#endif\n",
    })

    # 9. the allowlist actually silences a divergence -> GREEN
    case("allowlisted divergence is tolerated", False, {
        "a/a.h": "#define BLOCK_SIZE 512\n",
        "b/b.c": "#define BLOCK_SIZE 4096\n",
    }, allow_rows=[("BLOCK_SIZE", "[LEGIT]",
                    "unrelated: one is an ATA sector, the other an ext2 block")])

    # 10. char and string literals are compared too
    case("differing string literals in two files", True, {
        "a/a.h": '#define BANNER "maytera"\n',
        "b/b.c": '#define BANNER "MAYTERA"\n',
    })

    # 11. a // comment after the value must not become part of it
    case("trailing comment is not part of the value", False, {
        "a/a.h": "#define TICK_HZ 100 // scheduler tick\n",
        "b/b.c": "#define TICK_HZ 100   /* same */\n",
    })

    # 12. a multi-line macro is not a literal
    case("multi-line macro ignored", False, {
        "a/a.h": "#define DO_IT 1 \\\n  + 2\n",
        "b/b.c": "#define DO_IT 4 \\\n  + 5\n",
    })

    # 13. negative numbers compare correctly
    case("-1 vs -0x1 are the same; -1 vs 1 are not", True, {
        "a/a.h": "#define ERRV -1\n",
        "b/b.c": "#define ERRV -0x1\n",
        "c/c.c": "#define ERRV 1\n",
    })

    # ---- RULE C: value contracts across DIFFERENTLY NAMED constants ----------
    # This is the #222 fault itself. settings/main.c and compositor/taskbar.c
    # hold the same dock geometry under DIFFERENT names, so no name-anchored
    # rule can ever see the pair. These four cases are the proof that the
    # declared-contract path covers it, including the drift that rules A and B
    # are structurally blind to.
    def contract_case(desc, want_red, files, rows):
        nonlocal passed, total
        total += 1
        d = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="dupconst-ct-")
        saved = globals()["CONTRACTS"]
        try:
            for rel, body in files.items():
                p = os.path.join(d, rel)
                os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(p), exist_ok=True)
                with open(p, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                    fh.write(body)
            cf = os.path.join(d, "contracts.tsv")
            with open(cf, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                for r in rows:
                    fh.write("\t".join(r) + "\n")
            globals()["CONTRACTS"] = cf
            errs = []
            cs = load_contracts(errs)
            check_contracts(d, cs, errs)
            red = bool(errs)
            if red == want_red:
                print("  %-5s (correct)  %s" % ("RED" if red else "GREEN", desc))
                passed += 1
            else:
                print("  %-5s (WRONG!)   %s  %s" % ("RED" if red else "GREEN", desc, errs))
        finally:
            globals()["CONTRACTS"] = saved
            shutil.rmtree(d, ignore_errors=True)

    DOCK_SITES = ("settings/main.c:DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT;"
                  "compositor/taskbar.c:XFCE_DOCK_H_DEFAULT")
    WHY = "the dock the compositor draws is the dock settings configures"
    TASKBAR_OK = ("#define XFCE_DOCK_ICON 40\n#define XFCE_DOCK_PAD 8\n"
                  "#define XFCE_DOCK_IND 8\n#define XFCE_DOCK_PAD_BOT 3\n"
                  "#define XFCE_DOCK_H_DEFAULT (XFCE_DOCK_ICON + XFCE_DOCK_PAD"
                  " + XFCE_DOCK_IND + XFCE_DOCK_PAD_BOT)  // 59\n")

    contract_case("differently-named dock constants agree, one is a 4-term sum", False, {
        "settings/main.c": "#define DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT 59\n",
        "compositor/taskbar.c": TASKBAR_OK,
    }, [("dock.height.default", "59", DOCK_SITES, WHY)])

    contract_case("one side drifts to 62 - INVISIBLE to rules A and B, RED here", True, {
        "settings/main.c": "#define DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT 62\n",
        "compositor/taskbar.c": TASKBAR_OK,
    }, [("dock.height.default", "59", DOCK_SITES, WHY)])

    contract_case("a term inside the sum drifts (PAD_BOT 3 -> 6)", True, {
        "settings/main.c": "#define DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT 59\n",
        "compositor/taskbar.c": TASKBAR_OK.replace("PAD_BOT 3", "PAD_BOT 6"),
    }, [("dock.height.default", "59", DOCK_SITES, WHY)])

    contract_case("a contract site that no longer exists is STALE, not silently skipped", True, {
        "settings/main.c": "#define DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT 59\n",
    }, [("dock.height.default", "59", DOCK_SITES, WHY)])

    contract_case("a contract naming only one site is refused", True, {
        "settings/main.c": "#define DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT 59\n",
    }, [("dock.height.default", "59", "settings/main.c:DOCK_HEIGHT_DEFAULT", WHY)])

    # 14. allowlist validation: a status that is not one of the three FAILS
    total += 1
    errs = []
    saved = globals()["ALLOWLIST"]
    import tempfile as _tf
    fd, tmp = _tf.mkstemp()
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as fh:
        fh.write("FOO\t[MAYBE]\tsome long enough reason text goes right here ok\n")
        fh.write("BAR\t[LEGIT]\tshort\n")
        fh.write("BAZ\t[LEGIT]\ta properly long and specific justification here\n")
    globals()["ALLOWLIST"] = tmp
    got = load_allowlist(errs)
    globals()["ALLOWLIST"] = saved
    os.unlink(tmp)
    if len(errs) == 2 and "BAZ" in got and "FOO" not in got and "BAR" not in got:
        print("  RED   (correct)  bad status and empty justification both rejected")
        passed += 1
    else:
        print("  WRONG!  allowlist validation: errs=%d got=%s" % (len(errs), sorted(got)))

    print("\nSELF-TEST: %d/%d correct" % (passed, total))
    return 0 if passed == total else 1


def main(argv):
    if "--self-test" in argv:
        return self_test()
    root = None
    dirs = []
    strict = twins = report = False
    i = 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a == "--dirs":
            i += 1
            dirs.append(argv[i])
        elif a == "--strict":
            strict = True
        elif a == "--twins":
            twins = True
        elif a == "--report":
            report = True
        elif a in ("-h", "--help"):
            print(__doc__)
            return 0
        elif a.startswith("-"):
            print("unknown option %s" % a, file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        else:
            root = a
        i += 1
    if root is None:
        root = "."
    root = os.path.abspath(root)
    if not os.path.isdir(root):
        print("[dup-const-lint] FATAL: no such root %s" % root, file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    return run(root, dirs, strict, twins, report)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
